"EAU: Environment-Agent-User Co-evolution for Training User-Centric Agents (https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.16523)\nciteturn69academia12 [wordlim: 200] Published: 3 days ago; We instead introduce EAU, a meta-agent framework for environment--agent--user co-evolution.\nTitle: EAU: Environment-Agent-User Co-evolution for Training User-Centric Agents\nAuthors: Min Cai, Dan Zhang, Gao Xing, Libin Ma, Zhaohui Xu, Xi Ye\nDate: Mon Oct 12 10:11:38 2026\n\nTraining LLM agents to interact with users over multiple turns is challenging because the learning signal depends not only on the agent, but also on the user simulator and environment, which jointly shape the agent's experience. Simulated users can be stochastic or inconsistent, while environments may contain unreliable tools, incomplete state coverage, and incorrect verifiers. Prior work mainly addresses the resulting instability through reward shaping or denser supervision, while treating these components as fixed. We instead introduce EAU, a meta-agent framework for environment--agent--user co-evolution. During training, EAU monitors trajectories, tool traces, rewards, and training statistics; diagnoses failures and uninformative interactions; and proposes targeted modifications to the user simulator, environment, reward design, and RL configuration. Validated changes are hot-swapped into the ongoing run, allowing the training setup to evolve together with the policy. These adaptations also persist across runs, so later training can build on previously discovered improvements rather than restarting from a fixed setup. We enable this process through an editable symbolic representation of user and environment dynamics, while the agent remains a neural LLM optimized with RL. Across three interactive-agent benchmarks and two backbone models, EAU consistently outperforms prior RL methods, improving the overall score over the strongest baseline by 14.7--15.0% and increasing successful task completion on ColBench by up to 36.7%, showing that co-evolving the training system with the policy can substantially improve multi-turn RL.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nMuse: Meta's personal AI agent, features & capabilities (https://ai.meta.com/muse/)\nciteturn69search0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: last month; # Your personal AI agent that gets things done ... Eligible purchases are covered by Link’s purchase protections, a first for AI agents, and your conversations are not shared with Meta's ad systems. ... Muse is built with automated protections, agentic AI security standards, and user controls designed to prevent harmful content and unauthorized actions.\n\n## Introducing Muse\n\n# Your personal AI agent that gets things done\n\nImage\n\n## Introducing Muse\n\n# Your personal AI agent that gets things done\n\n## Introducing Muse\n\n# Your personal AI agent that gets things done\n\nScroll to explore\n\n## Take the busywork off your plate\n\nMuse gets to know you and your goals, working across all aspects of life, so you can focus on the things that matter. It comes with strong privacy and security built in, so you stay in control.\n\n\n### A secure computer with its own browser\n\nMuse Secure VM runs on a persistent, dedicated virtual machine equipped with a state-of-the-art browser to navigate the web. It books appointments, fills out forms and handles customer service.\n\nMuse Secure VM runs on a persistent, dedicated virtual machine equipped with a state-of-the-art browser to navigate the web.\nIt books appointments, fills out forms and handles customer service.\n\n\n### Designed to feel like a conversation\n\nChat with your Muse in the same way you message with other people, using the Muse app or directly in WhatsApp. It’s simple, just tell Muse what you want to get done.\n\nImage: Muse asking for approval to purchase something you were looking for\n\n### Approve critical actions from your agent\n\nReview and approve actions from Muse before they happen, like sending emails and making purchases. See a complete audit trail of everything your agent has done (and what it is planning to do).\n\n### Your personal data is safe and secure\n\nYour logins are kept in a secure credential store your agent can't read, with 1Password integration coming soon. When shopping, a one-time card number is generated at checkout so your real card is never seen by the merchant or your agent. Eligible purchases are covered by Link’s purchase protections, a first for AI agents, and your conversations are not shared with Meta's ad systems.\n\nImage: Goals and ideas tracker\n\n### Always working on your goals and suggesting ideas\n\nGive Muse a goal or a task and it builds an action plan, tracks your progress and proactively takes tasks off your plate along the way. It learns and gets sharper, coming back with ideas and suggestions built around what you need, ready to act on them under your direction.\n\n\n### Works with your apps and builds its own tools\n\nConnect your email, calendar, Instagram and more apps you use daily. If a task needs a tool that doesn't exist, Muse builds it for you.\n\n## PRODUCT WALKTHROUGH\n\n### Get the most out of Muse\n\nWatch how to hand off more to your agent, connect what matters and keep progress moving.\n\nTry Muse\n\n0:00 / 0:00\n\n\n## TRY MUSE\n\n### Ready to get more done with Muse?\n\nGet started\n\n## FREQUENTLY ASKED QUESTIONS\n\n* * *\n\n### What can Muse do?\n\n  * Muse can answer questions, complete tasks, browse the web, make purchases, generate images, create documents, and connect with your favorite apps and services via Connectors. It can also set reminders, track goals, and monitor things you care about, all working in the background on your behalf.\n\nYes. When you ask Muse to do something, it keeps working in the background whether you're actively using the app or not. For example, if you ask it to monitor daily weather reports and alert you about rain, it will do that on its own without you needing to check in.\n\n* * *\n\n### Does Muse ask before it acts on my behalf?\n\nYes. Muse is designed to ask your permission before taking certain actions, like sending messages, making purchases, or sharing information with a connected app. You can choose to allow an action once, always allow it, or deny it. You can manage your default permission settings for every connection at any time.\n\n* * *\n\n### What is a personal AI agent?\n\nA personal AI agent is an AI that can take actions and get things done on your behalf, not just answer questions. Muse, for example, can browse the web, connect to your apps, complete multi-step tasks, and keep working in the background with your direction and oversight.\n\n* * *\n\n### How is a personal agent different from a chatbot or AI assistant?\n\nA chatbot responds to one question at a time. An AI assistant can handle more complex conversations and generate content. A personal AI agent like Muse goes further: it can take real actions across the web and connected apps, work through multi-step tasks on its own, and keep running in the background even after you close the app.\n\n* * *\n\n### What can a personal AI agent help with?\n\nA personal AI agent can help with everyday tasks like booking reservations, monitoring prices, managing reminders, creating documents, generating images, and researching topics. It can also connect to third-party apps and services to take actions on your behalf, like sending emails or completing online purchases. See more agentic AI examples.\n\n\n## Explore Meta AI products\n\nImage: Meta AI personal assistant on mobile device\n\n### Meta AI Assistant\n\n#### Get more done with Meta AI, your personal AI assistant.\n\nTry Meta AI\n\nLearn more\n\nImage: Meta AI media generation output cards\n\n### Image Generation\n\n#### Explore fun presets, make precise edits and visualize your ideas.\n\nStart generating--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nBe There for Every Customer With Meta Business Agent (https://about.fb.com/news/2026/06/meta-business-agent?trk=article-ssr-frontend-pulse_x-social-details_comments-action_comment-text)\nciteturn69search1 [wordlim: 200] Published: 4 months ago; Crawled: 4 days ago;   * We’re introducing Meta Business Agent, which lets businesses of all sizes increase their output and deliver personalized experiences for customers using AI. ...   * We’re also introducing the Meta Business Agent Platform, which gives businesses the infrastructure to build, customize, and deploy their Business Agents at scale.\n\nBack to Newsroom\n\nMeta\n\n# Be There for Every Customer With Meta Business Agent\n\nJune 3, 2026June 3, 2026\n\nListen to Article 10 Pause 10\n\n[Select]\n\nImage\n\n## Takeaways\n\n  * We’re introducing Meta Business Agent, which lets businesses of all sizes increase their output and deliver personalized experiences for customers using AI.\n  * Business Agent also doubles as a partner to deliver a morning briefing to catch you up on chats you missed overnight and provide insights on your threads.\n  * We’re also introducing the Meta Business Agent Platform, which gives businesses the infrastructure to build, customize, and deploy their Business Agents at scale.\n\nToday, we’re introducing Meta Business Agent – AI that lets every business show up for every customer as if they had an infinite team behind them. Business Agent can be set up in minutes or plugged directly into your existing enterprise infrastructure so you can 10X or 100X output.\n\n \n\nMore than one million businesses are already using a Meta Business Agent on WhatsApp and Messenger to respond to customers around the clock. \n## An AI Agent That Works for You\n\nBecause the Meta Business Agent responds to customers, it doubles as a partner that can deliver a morning briefing to catch you up on chats you missed overnight and provide insights on your threads. We’re starting with a select number of businesses on the WhatsApp Business app, Instagram Pro, Messenger, and Meta Business Suite, and in the future we’ll expand its capabilities to help fully run all your daily operations — like conducting market research, surfacing product insights, connecting with the tools to manage your calendar and providing competitive intelligence. You can join the waitlist here.\n\n## Personalizing Customer Experiences\n\nWe’re also introducing the Meta Business Agent Platform: a new agentic platform that gives businesses the infrastructure to build, customize, and deploy their Business Agent at scale. It lets businesses connect to a growing suite of hundreds of systems like Shopify, Zendesk, and Shopee giving Business Agents the ability to take action on behalf of the business. --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nmeta-agent/README.md at main · canvas-org/meta-agent · GitHub (https://github.com/canvas-org/meta-agent/blob/main/README.md)\nciteturn69search2 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 3 weeks ago; # Meta-AgentContinual learning for agents, without weight updates.Meta-Agent turns execution traces into persistent improvements to the system around a language model. ...             messages=[{\"role\": \"user\", \"content\": str(ctx.task)}],\n\n# Meta-Agent\n\nContinual learning for agents, without weight updates.\n\nMeta-Agent turns execution traces into persistent improvements to the system around a language model. It learns at two timescales:\n\n  * a fast loop improves the task-facing harness, including prompts, tools, hooks, stopping criteria, subagents, and control flow;\n  * an experimental slow loop improves the proposer skill that diagnoses failures and generates future harness changes.\n\nThe base model remains frozen. Meta-Agent keeps what it learns in harness code and versioned proposer instructions, backed by an on-disk experience store.\n\n> Research question: Can an agent improve both its harness and the process used to improve that harness?\n\nRead the technical report →\n\nMethod · Result · Quickstart\n\n## Overview\n\nAn agent is more than its model. The harness decides what context the model sees, which tools it can call, how it tracks state, when it checks its output, and when it stops.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nEAU: Environment-Agent-User Co-evolution for Training User-Centric Agents · Pith (https://pith.science/paper/2610.16523)\nciteturn69search3 [wordlim: 200] Published: 4 days ago; Crawled: yesterday; \"EAU: Environment-Agent-User Co-evolution for Training User-Centric Agents.\" arXiv:2610.16523 (2026). https://pith.science/paper/EBSLUJDR ... If the reported gains were attributable to the co-evolution mechanism rather than to an uncontrolled compute advantage, the idea of continuously adapting the training system during RL would be a useful and novel contribution. ... [§5.2 and §5.3, Table 1] The meta-agent is instantiated with Claude Opus-5, a proprietary frontier model that is not available to the research community for independent verification or reproduction. ... 10 Preprint:EAU, A Meta-Agent forEnvironment-Agent-User Co-evolution AI USESTATEMENT Generative AI is a functional component of the proposed method and experimental pipeline.” ... Consider moving it to the main text for clarity about the evaluation protocol.\n## Why Pith says this\n\nHighlight any text to comment on it.\n\nEditor's note read the letter\n\nThe paper's real contribution is the meta-agent framework that dynamically modifies the user simulator, environment rewards, and RL hyperparameters during training, with hot-swapping and cross-run experience reuse. That combination is new. The experiments are thorough: three benchmarks, two backbones, consistent gains (up to 36.7% on ColBench). The authors also document interventions transparently and acknowledge limitations candidly. This is a serious engineering effort.\n\nThe soft spot that matters most is the unmatched compute budget. The paper itself says (around b125) that its limits 'specify the rollout and search setup, rather than a matched end-to-end compute budget'. In practice, EAU uses a frontier meta-agent (Claude Opus-5), performs up to 5 runs with proactive restarts, and hot-swaps modifications — all extra computation that baselines like InfoPO do not get. The ablation (Table 4) shows that a generic meta-agent without the EAU-specific skill produces mixed results, but that baseline also lacks matched compute. The main recommendation for revision: provide a matched-budget comparison (e.g., give baselines the same number of total environment steps plus meta-agent inference cost), report multiple full training repetitions, and ideally include at least one open-source meta-agent result.\n\nWhat would settle it\n\nIf the same EAU framework were run with a different meta-agent that performs random knob perturbations (or no meta-agent at all) and the performance gain disappeared, it would show that the meta-agent's diagnostic and proposal quality is essential, and the gain is not simply due to searching a broader space of configurations.\n\nReferee report\n\nSignificance\n\nIf the reported gains were attributable to the co-evolution mechanism rather than to an uncontrolled compute advantage, the idea of continuously adapting the training system during RL would be a useful and novel contribution. The paper documents a rich set of intervention interfaces and records their use, which provides transparency about what the meta-agent actually changes. The symbolic user and the hot-swap mechanism are well-motivated. However, as I detail in Major Comments, the current evidence does not isolate the mechanism from the extra compute budget and the proprietary meta-agent, and the quantitative claims in the abstract and main text are stronger than the data support.\n\nMajor objections (5)\n\n  1. [§5.2 and §5.3, Table 1] The paper explicitly states in §5.2 that the training budgets 'specify the rollout and search setup, rather than a matched end-to-end compute budget; the latter would also need to account for meta-agent inference and all attempted training runs.' This is a significant limitation. The main results in Table 1 compare EAU — which uses Claude Opus-5 for meta-agent inference, performs up to 5 runs with proactive restarts, and hot-swaps modifications — against baselines like PPO or InfoPO that receive none of this additional orchestration. The claim of 14.7–15.0% improvement therefore conflates the method's design with an uncontrolled compute and search budget. Showing 7 of the paper's 8 figures. See all figures in the PDF ↗ Figures reproduced from arXiv:2610.16523 by the authors.\n\nReferences\n\nWorks cited (24)\n\n  1. [3]\n\nZhenyu Hou, Yujiang Li, Jie Tang, and Yuxiao Dong. Single-rollout asynchronous optimization for agentic reinforcement learning.arXiv preprint arXiv:2607.07508,\n\nView on Pith Pith review arXiv\n\n  2. [4]\n\nURLhttps://arxiv.org/abs/2608.19880. Carlos E. Jimenez, John Yang, Alexander Wettig, Shunyu Yao, Kexin Pei, Ofir Press, and Karthik Narasimhan. Swe-bench: Can language models resolve real-world github issues?,\n\nView on Pith Pith review arXiv\n\n  3. [6]\n\nURL https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.07925. Fanqi Kong, Jiayi Zhang, Mingyi Deng, Chenglin Wu, Yuyu Luo, and Bang Liu. Infopo: Information-driven policy optimization for user-centric agents,\n\nView on Pith Pith review arXiv\n\n  4. [7]\n\nURLhttps://arxiv. org/abs/2603.00656. Yoonho Lee, Roshen Nair, Qizheng Zhang, Kangwook Lee, Omar Khattab, and Chelsea Finn. Meta- harness: End-to-end optimization of model harnesses,\n\nView on Pith Pith review arXiv\n\n  5. [8]\n\nURLhttps://arxiv.org/ abs/2603.28052. 11 Preprint:EAU, A Meta-Agent forEnvironment-Agent-User Co-evolution Siyi Liu, Chen Gao, and Yong Li. Large language model agent for hyper-parameter optimization. arXiv preprint arXiv:2402.01881,\n\nView on Pith Pith review arXiv\n\n  6. [9]\n\nURLhttps://arxiv.org/abs/ 2606.01770. Alexander Panfilov, Peter Romov, Igor Shilov, Yves-Alexandre de Montjoye, Jonas Geiping, and Maksym Andriushchenko. Claudini: Autoresearch discovers state-of-the-art adversarial attack algorithms for llms.arXiv preprint arXiv:2603.24511,\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nGraphical User Interface - Meta Agents Research Environments (https://facebookresearch.github.io/meta-agents-research-environments/user_guide/understanding_ui.html)\nciteturn69search4 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: last month; # Graphical User Interface¶ ... This guide is for the Meta Agents Research Environments (ARE) UI, explaining the full simulation tool interface, features and components. ... You can also view the agent logs following a conversation with the agent.\n\n# Graphical User Interface¶\n\n## Introduction¶\n\nThis guide is for the Meta Agents Research Environments (ARE) UI, explaining the full simulation tool interface, features and components. In the ARE UI, you will be able to run different models on a fixed baseline orchestration and environment, observe agent behavior, and explore Gaia2 environments and scenarios. Reference this guide to help you understand how to get started with ARE UI.\n\n## Getting Started¶\n\nUpon entering the ARE UI, you will be greeted by an introductory screen that provides an overview explanation of the different workspaces, along with links to documentation. There are two workspaces in the ARE UI: Playground and Scenarios. See below for details on each of these workspaces. The documentation resources will help you learn more about the ARE application itself as well as the Gaia2 benchmark. You can access this screen again anytime by clicking [about] at the bottom of the left navigation pane.\n\nClick [Got it] to proceed to the Scenario View\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nAgent frameworks - Meta Model API (https://dev.meta.ai/